{"qid":"Q43287","names":["German Empire"],"title":"German Empire","endonym":{"native":"Deutsches Reich","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The German Empire (Deutsches Reich) was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871 following Prussia's defeat of France in the Franco-Prussian War, unifying twenty-five German states under the Hohenzollern kaisers with Chancellor Otto von Bismarck as its architect. At its industrial peak in the 1900s Germany led Europe in steel production, chemical manufacture, and railway density, while Bismarck's treaty system briefly managed the European balance of power before his dismissal by Kaiser Wilhelm II in 1890. Wilhelm II's erratic foreign policy, naval buildup, and colonial ambitions contributed to the diplomatic alignments that produced the First World War; after four years of stalemated industrial warfare, a naval mutiny and revolution in November 1918 forced Wilhelm II to abdicate, and the republic was proclaimed on 9 November 1918, formally ending the empire.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The German Empire's boundaries are precisely defined by the treaties of 1871 and subsequent agreements; Alsace-Lorraine, colonial holdings, and minor frontier rectifications are all treaty-documented, and the dataset geometry is reliable for European territory.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: German Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/German-Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Germany — The German Empire 1871–1914","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Germany/The-German-Empire-1871-1914"}],"britannica":"place/German-Empire","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"German Empi